# SwiftUI/macOS Standards

## Architecture

- Keep Core layer (Models, ViewModels, Services) platform-agnostic for future iOS port
- macOS UI lives under `Schema/macOS/` only
- Use MVVM: Views observe ViewModels, ViewModels own business logic

## SwiftData

- Use `@Model` macro for all persistent types
- Use `@Query` for fetching in views, never raw `ModelContext.fetch()` in views
- Relationships must specify `@Relationship` with explicit delete rules
- Use `@Bindable` for `@Model` objects in views requiring mutation

## View Performance

- Extract heavy computations to ViewModels
- Avoid `AnyView` — use `@ViewBuilder` or concrete types
- Avoid unnecessary structural view rebuilds; pass stable identifiers

## Forbidden Patterns

- No UIKit bridging (use pure SwiftUI or AppKit where required)
- No deprecated modifiers: `.navigationBarTitle`, `.edgesIgnoringSafeArea`
- No `GeometryReader` for simple layouts
- No hacky view-hierarchy workarounds or private API usage

## macOS Native

- Use `List` with `.listStyle(.inset)` or `.listStyle(.plain)`
- Use `TextField` with `.textFieldStyle(.roundedBorder)`
- Use standard window chrome, no custom title bars
- Keyboard shortcuts via `.keyboardShortcut()` and `.onKeyPress()`

## User Edits

- When the user has manually edited a file, preserve their changes. Do not revert, overwrite, or reformat unrelated parts of manually modified code unless explicitly asked.

## Examples

```swift
// BAD — fetch in view
let tasks = try? modelContext.fetch(FetchDescriptor<SchemaTask>())

// GOOD — declarative query
@Query(sort: \SchemaTask.createdAt) private var tasks: [SchemaTask]
```

```swift
// BAD — platform logic in view
UNUserNotificationCenter.current().add(request)

// GOOD — service abstraction
await notificationService.scheduleReminder(for: task)
```
